  "title": "Adjective Endings",
  "explanation": "Adjectives placed before a noun take an ending that depends on the noun's gender, case, and whether it follows a definite or indefinite article. After a definite article (der/die/das) the endings are simpler — usually **-e** or **-en**; after an indefinite article (ein/eine) they carry more of the gender information themselves.",
  "examples": [
    { "de": "der große Mann", "en": "the tall man" },
    { "de": "ein großer Mann", "en": "a tall man" },
    { "de": "die kleine Katze", "en": "the small cat" }
  ],
